{"choices":[{"text":"<think>\n\n</think>\n\n# Technical Explanation of B-Tree Indexes in Relational Databases\n\nA B-tree (Balanced Tree) index is the most common indexing structure used in relational database management systems (RDBMS) such as PostgreSQL, MySQL (InnoDB), Oracle, and SQL Server. It is designed to provide efficient access to data in a sorted manner, supporting both exact match queries and range scans.\n\nBelow is a detailed technical breakdown of how a B-tree index works, covering its structure, insertion mechanics (including splits), and range scan operations.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Core Concept and Purpose\n\nThe primary goal of a B-tree index is to reduce the number of disk I/O operations required to locate a specific record or a range of records. By organizing data in a balanced tree structure, the database ensures that the depth of the tree (and thus the number of I/Os) is logarithmic with respect to the number of records.\n\n- **Time Complexity:** $O(\\log N)$ for search, insert, and delete operations.\n- **Space Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of indexed rows.\n- **Balance Property:** All leaf nodes are at the same depth, ensuring consistent performance regardless of which key is accessed.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Node Structure\n\nA B-tree consists of nodes connected by pointers. Each node has a fixed capacity defined by the database’s page size (typically 8KB or 16KB). The structure varies slightly between \"internal\" (non-leaf) nodes and \"leaf\" nodes.\n\n### 2.1. Internal Nodes (Non-Leaf Nodes)\n\nInternal nodes act as routing structures. They do not store the actual data records but contain **keys** and **pointers** to child nodes.\n\n- **Keys:** Each key in an internal node represents the **maximum key value** in the subtree rooted by the pointer to its right.","index":0,"logprobs":{"content":[{"id":248068,"token":"<think>","bytes":[60,116,104,105,110,107,62],"logprob":-3.576279254957626e-07,"top_logprobs":[{"id":248068,"token":"<think>"